May 11th 2015 the GOI one satellite  captures a strange marking across the  arid plains of Bolivia what would create  a dark streak like that out in the  desert  there's no way to relate the darkened in  areas to any phenomenon that I can think  of the answer may lie in the mining  history of mineral-rich Bolivia and  neighboring Chile the Atacama Desert is  very dry rocky area almost no plant  growth very few animals there is however  some industrial development there  because of the minerals that exist the  region from the Atacama Desert to the  Bolivian Plains was once famous for its  wealth of tin copper silver and nitrate  reserves there is a long history of  mining in the Atacama starting with  exploitation of the nitrate deposits in  the early part of the nineteenth century  I understand that much of the gunpowder  used by the British and allied forces in  World War one that was manufactured with  nitrate from the Atacama in the late  1800s a vast network of railroads was  built to support the region's booming  mining industry  but in the 1930s disaster struck as the  Great Depression wiped out demand the  trains were left to rot in the desert  using image enhancement the identity of  the dark mark visible from space is  finally revealed  what I am seeing is what I would call a  train graveyard it is hundreds of trains  there just littering the earth in this  designated cemetery  these are the trains that built the  Bolivian Wild West  you
 

